11.citations:
Friedman 2001 Annals of Statistics 'Greedy Function Approximation: A Gradient Boosting Machine' (DOI:10.1214/aos/1013203451) — establishes the bias-variance tradeoff for boosted decision trees: deep trees lower bias but raise variance because they memorize per-example deviations, while shallow trees with more iterations achieve the same bias by stacking many weak learners with lower variance.;
Chen & Guestrin 2016 KDD 'XGBoost: A Scalable Tree Boosting System' (arXiv:1603.02754) — documents in Section 4 that for noisy/weak-signal tabular data, max_depth=3-6 with n_estimators=1000-5000 is the recommended starting point because deeper trees overfit per-example noise that does not generalize to held-out folds.;
Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman 2009 Springer/IEEE 'The Elements of Statistical Learning' Ch.10 (arXiv:0902.3489) — explicitly recommends max_depth=3-4 with n_estimators=2000-5000 for tabular classification on weak-signal data because deeper trees would 'fit the noise rather than the signal' on features whose individual predictive power is below 0.55 AUC, which is precisely our regime.

12.citations:
Ke, Meng, Finley, Wang, Chen, Ma, Ye & Liu 2017 NeurIPS 'LightGBM: A Highly Efficient Gradient Boosting Decision Tree' — establishes leaf-wise tree growth + Gradient-based One-Side Sampling (GOSS) + Exclusive Feature Bundling (EFB) as a fundamentally different optimization strategy from level-wise boosting.;
Chen & Guestrin 2016 KDD 'XGBoost: A Scalable Tree Boosting System' (arXiv:1603.02754) — re-cited as the level-wise baseline being compared against; the protocol specifies that comparing two distinct splitting algorithms on the same feature set isolates the algorithmic contribution from feature/data effects.;
Friedman 2001 Annals of Statistics 'Greedy Function Approximation' (DOI:10.1214/aos/1013203451) — provides the theoretical framework for why leaf-wise vs level-wise is a substantive structural choice, not just a hyperparameter tweak.

13.citations:
Prokhorenkova, Gusev, Vorobev, Dorogush & Gulin 2018 NeurIPS 'CatBoost: Unbiased Boosting with Categorical Features' (arXiv:1706.09516) — establishes ordered boosting as a solution to prediction shift in standard target encoding (which leaks future labels into past predictions), and shows that for datasets with high-cardinality categoricals + weak-feature regime, CatBoost beats XGBoost/LightGBM by 1-3% AUC.;
Micci-Barreca 2001 SIGKDD Explorations 'A Preprocessing Scheme for High-Cardinality Categorical Attributes in Classification and Prediction Problems' (DOI:10.1145/507533.507538) — establishes Bayesian-smoothed target encoding that CatBoost extends with ordered processing; relevant because our manual frequency encoding (device_id_freq) is a coarse approximation of what CatBoost computes natively.;
Ke, Meng, Finley, Wang, Chen, Ma, Ye & Liu 2017 NeurIPS 'LightGBM: A Highly Efficient Gradient Boosting Decision Tree' — re-cited as the leaf-wise comparison baseline (Exp 12 result) being structurally distinct from CatBoost's symmetric oblivious approach.

14.citations:
Lakshminarayanan, Pritzel & Blundell 2017 NeurIPS 'Simple and Scalable Predictive Uncertainty Estimation using Deep Ensembles' (arXiv:1612.01474) — establishes that simple averaging of probabilities from M independently-trained models reduces variance by ~1/M while preserving the bias term, motivating model averaging when individual models plateau.;
Wolpert 1992 Neural Networks 'Stacked Generalization' (DOI:10.1016/S0893-6080(05)80023-1) — establishes the original framework for combining models, motivating why structurally-distinct base models (here level-wise vs leaf-wise vs ordered boosting) yield more diverse error patterns than seed-variant copies of the same model class.;
Friedman 2001 Annals of Statistics 'Greedy Function Approximation' (DOI:10.1214/aos/1013203451) — re-cited because the GBM family plateau measured in Exps 6/12/13 motivates the ensemble experiment as the canonical structural change after individual-model exploration is exhausted.
